---
.title = "Argument parsing and return values",
.date = .date("2026-09-20T00:00:00"),
.layout = "doc.shtml",
.translation_key = "reference/registration/arguments",
.custom = { "section": "Declarations and registration" },
---

Functions, methods and closures use the same typed handler signatures. Declare arguments and the return type in Zig and use a trailing `phpz.Variadic` for variadic arguments. Use `Ctx` or `GuardCtx` to parse arguments manually if you need constraints on referenced values, specific classes or a type-checked zval view. Keep either signature consistent with the PHP stub.

# [Arguments and return values]($heading.id('arguments-and-return-values'))

The following handler implements `function greet(string $name): string`. For binding details, see [Function bindings]($link.page('reference/registration/functions')).

```zig
pub fn greet(name: []const u8) []const u8 {
    return name;
}
```

The wrapper checks arguments before calling the handler and sets the PHP return value on success. The input slice borrows the argument's bytes. The wrapper copies the returned slice. The equivalent explicit specification is:

```zig
pub fn greet(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
    const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
        .{ .string = .{} },
    }, {});
    ctx.ret(.string, args[0]);
}
```

`expectArgs` derives the argument count from the specification and returns a typed tuple. Scalar arguments follow Zend parameter parsing (ZPP) conversion rules, just as they do in typed signatures. See [Optional and nullable arguments]($link.ref('missing-and-null')).

`ctx.call.arg(1)` borrows the first argument. With this low-level interface, check the count and type before accessing the value.

Both `Ctx` and `GuardCtx` provide `ctx.ret(kind, value)`, shorthand for `ctx.retval.set(kind, value)`. It accepts the same types as [Ownership and reference counting]($link.page('reference/types/ownership')). `ctx.retval` is a `*phpz.Zval` pointing to the PHP return slot. Use `ctx.retval.ptr()` with lower-level APIs.

`ret` writes the return slot and execution continues. Use `return ctx.ret(.int, 42);` to exit the Zig function immediately.

# [Typed handler signatures]($heading.id('typed-signatures'))

An optional `Ctx` or `GuardCtx` precedes all PHP arguments. For ordinary instance methods on a class with Zig fields, `self` comes first. A context-only handler checks its own arguments. For typed arguments the wrapper parses the entire signature and rejects excess arguments unless it ends in `Variadic`.

| Zig parameter | PHP declaration | Input and behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `i64` / `isize` | `int $a` | Parse a PHP int and check the Zig range. Overflow raises an argument `ValueError` |
| `u8` / `u16` / `u32` / `u64` / `usize` | `int $a` | Parse a PHP int and check the Zig range. Negative or out-of-range values raise an argument `ValueError` |
| `f32` / `f64` | `float $a` | Parse a PHP float. Conversion to `f32` checks finite values for overflow and can lose precision |
| `bool` | `bool $a` | Parse a PHP bool |
| `[]const u8` | `string $a` | Parse a PHP string and borrow its bytes |
| `[:0]const u8` | `string $a` | Borrow a NUL-terminated slice, preserving the length and embedded NULs |
| `*phpz.zend.String` | `string $a` | Parse a PHP string and borrow the Zend string |
| `*phpz.zend.Array` | `array $a` | Borrow the PHP array |
| `*phpz.zend.Object` | `object $a` | Borrow the PHP object |
| `*phpz.zend.Resource` | `@param resource $a` (PHPDoc) | Borrow the PHP resource |
| `*phpz.Stream` | `@param resource $a` (PHPDoc) | Check for a regular or persistent stream resource and borrow the stream |
| `*phpz.zend.Callable` | `callable $a` | Resolve the callable. The temporary resolution is valid only during this invocation |
| `*phpz.zend.Reference` | `mixed &$a` | Borrow the PHP reference. Use a manual `.reference.kind` to constrain the referenced value |
| `*phpz.Zval` | `mixed $a` | Borrow the argument zval, accepting any PHP value including null |
| `phpz.Mixed(&.{ .int, .string })` | `int\|string $a` | Parse into a tagged union |
| `?T` | `int $a = 0` | The argument may be omitted. The outer `null` means it was absent |
| `phpz.Nullable(T)` | `?int $a` / `int\|null $a` | Accept explicit PHP null, represented by `.null` or otherwise by `.value` |
| `?phpz.Nullable(T)` | `?int $a = null` | Accept omission and explicit `null` |
| `phpz.Variadic` | `mixed ...$a` | Must come last. Borrows remaining positional and extra named arguments and validates entries on demand |

The three `T` rows illustrate Zig `i64` and PHP `int`. A Zig `?T` permits omission. To accept explicit PHP null, choose a type that supports it. Handle stub defaults in the Zig implementation. A variadic declaration can constrain its elements, for example `int ...$a`. Check each with `Entry.expect()`.

PHP has no native `resource` parameter type. Document it in the stub with `@param resource`, as in [Borrowing a PHP stream]($link.page('reference/runtime/streams').ref('borrow-stream')).

`Nullable(T)` supports scalars, strings, arrays, objects, resources, `*phpz.Stream` and `*phpz.zend.Callable`. `*Zval` already accepts null, and `Mixed` can include `.null`. Use a manual specification to constrain the value inside a reference.

Scalar parsing follows these rules:

- In weak mode, ZPP supports conversions such as `"12"` to int, `1` to float and a Stringable object to string. Failed conversions raise `TypeError`.
- With `declare(strict_types=1)` in the caller, int to float remains valid. The other weak conversions above are rejected.
- Conversion updates the argument in the current call frame, including the value you see with `as = .zval`. Arrays, objects, resources, reference constraints and `Mixed` unions do not use scalar coercion.
- Non-nullable scalars follow the target PHP version's null handling. In PHP 8.2–8.6, weak calls emit a deprecation before conversion and strict calls reject null. Use `Nullable(T)` or `nullable = true` to preserve null.

`?T` makes an argument optional. If `T` itself accepts null, as mixed or a union containing `.null` does, it still accepts PHP null. Put optional arguments after required arguments.

## [Omitted and null arguments]($heading.id('typed-optional-example'))

For example, the following handler implements `function choose(?int $value = null): ?int`:

```zig
pub fn choose(value: ?phpz.Nullable(i64)) phpz.Nullable(i64) {
    return value orelse .{ .value = 0 };
}
```

```php
var_dump(choose());     // int(0)
var_dump(choose(null)); // NULL
var_dump(choose(42));   // int(42)
```

The outer `?` tells the handler whether the argument slot was supplied. For a supplied slot the inner `Nullable(i64)` carries either `.null` or `.value`. Here `orelse` runs only for the omitted argument. For calls that skip an earlier parameter by name, see [Named and variadic arguments]($link.ref('named-and-variadic-arguments')).

# [Typed unions]($heading.id('typed-unions'))

```zig
const IntOrString = phpz.Mixed(&.{ .int, .string });

pub fn describe(value: IntOrString) []const u8 {
    return switch (value) {
        .int => "integer",
        .string => "string",
    };
}
```

The matching PHP declaration is `function describe(int|string $value): string`. `Mixed` requires at least two distinct types. It can include `.null` and excludes `.mixed`, `.reference` and `.callable`. Use `phpz.Mixed`. A hand-written Zig union with similar fields is a different signature type.

# [Direct returns and ownership]($heading.id('typed-returns'))

Supported return types include the integers and floats listed above, bools, byte slices, pointers to Zend strings, arrays, objects and resources, `*zend.Reference`, `*Zval`, `Mixed`, `Nullable(T)`, optionals and their error unions. `Variadic` is input-only. With `void` or `!void`, use `ctx.ret()` to write a result. For other types let the wrapper set the PHP result from the Zig return value.

- Integers must fit in `zend_long` or conversion returns `ReturnValueOutOfRange`.
- The wrapper copies byte slices after the handler returns. Never return a slice of a stack buffer or memory released by `defer`. For local bytes, copy them inside the handler with `String.init` or call `ctx.ret(.string, bytes)` before returning.
- A returned `*zend.String`, `*zend.Array`, `*zend.Object`, `*zend.Resource` or `*zend.Reference` transfers one reference. Before returning a borrowed pointer, copy or retain it as described in [Ownership and reference counting]($link.page('reference/types/ownership')).
- Returning `*Zval` copies the dereferenced value. Its source retains ownership and must still be valid when the wrapper reads it.
- `*Stream` and `*zend.Callable` cannot be returned directly. To transfer a stream, call `toZval(ctx.retval)`.
- For direct returns, a PHP by-reference declaration (`function &name()`) requires `*zend.Reference` or its error union. A by-value declaration cannot return a reference directly. A context handler returning `void` or `!void` writes the return slot itself and must honor the declared reference semantics.

## [Returning a borrowed union value]($heading.id('return-borrowed-union'))

The active tag determines what PHP takes ownership of. In this example the handler retains a mutable array, object or resource for the return slot. An immutable array needs no increment. phpz copies the bytes of a string and writes scalar values directly.

```php
function keepValue(mixed $value): mixed {}
```

```zig
const Value = phpz.Mixed(&.{ .null, .bool, .int, .float, .string, .array, .object, .resource });

pub fn keepValue(value: Value) Value {
    switch (value) {
        .array => |array| if (!array.isImmutable()) array.addref(),
        inline .object, .resource => |ptr| ptr.addref(),
        else => {},
    }
    return value;
}
```

```php
$original = (object) ["name" => "phpz"];
$result = keepValue($original);
unset($original);
echo $result->name; // phpz
```

# [Manual parsing]($heading.id('manual-parsing'))

Use a context handler for `instanceof` checks, reference write-back, raw zvals or custom argument counts. `expectArgs` returns a typed tuple. The lower-level `parseArgs` uses PHP format strings and the corresponding conversion and output-pointer conventions.

## [Compile-time and Debug declaration checks]($heading.id('debug-declaration-checks'))

At compile time phpz compares typed handlers with arginfo for required and fixed parameter counts, variadic and parameter reference flags, and the reference flag on direct returns. A mismatch causes a compile error identifying the function or method. Keep PHP types and defaults in sync through stub regeneration and PHPT coverage. For context-only handlers, Zig Debug builds check the required and fixed counts and variadic flag against the current function's arginfo before each `expectArgs`. A mismatch terminates the process with a Zig panic reporting `expectArgs declaration mismatch`.

The runtime check excludes parameter types and defaults. ReleaseSafe, ReleaseFast and ReleaseSmall skip it regardless of the PHP build mode. Closures created by phpz with generic arginfo skip it as well. Closures derived from a registered function with its original declaration are checked. To parse a subset, use `expectArgCount` and `expectArg` and check all counts, types and extra named arguments yourself. For fixed by-reference parameters use `*zend.Reference`, its optional form, or raw `*Zval`. Direct returns follow the reference rules above.

# [Argument parsing types]($heading.id('argument-parsing-types'))

The first argument to `expectArgs` is an array of `ExpectArgKind.Spec`. The second supplies runtime data for specifications such as `callable` or `object instanceof`. Common options include:

| Option | Applies to | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `optional = true` | All except `.null` and `.variadic` | Allows omission and wraps the result in `?T` |
| `nullable = true` | Scalars, arrays, objects, resources and callables | Accepts explicit null and returns `phpz.Nullable(T)` unless `as = .zval` is selected |
| `as = .zval` | `.int`, `.float`, `.bool`, `.string`, `.array`, `.object`, `.resource` | Returns the borrowed `*Zval` |

Optional arguments must follow required ones. `.null` cannot stand alone as a parameter specification. Use `optional + nullable` or `.mixed` to distinguish omission, explicit null and a supplied value. `as` selects the output type. Numeric range checks match those for typed handlers.

These seven types support `optional`, `nullable` and `as`:

| Specification | Default Zig result | Ownership |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.int` | `i64` | Scalar value |
| `.float` | `f64` | Scalar value |
| `.bool` | `bool` | Scalar value |
| `.string` | `[]const u8` | Borrowed byte slice |
| `.array` | `*zend.Array` | Borrowed array |
| `.object` | `*zend.Object` | Borrowed object |
| `.resource` | `*zend.Resource` | Borrowed resource |

For a default result type `T`, combine options as follows:

| Options | Zig result | Explicit null |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Default | `T` | Non-nullable scalars follow ZPP as above. Other types reject null |
| `optional` | `?T` | Same as the default. Outer `null` means omission |
| `nullable` | `phpz.Nullable(T)` | `.null` for PHP null, `.value` otherwise |
| `optional + nullable` | `?phpz.Nullable(T)` | Outer `null` means omission. Inner `.null` means PHP null |
| `as = .zval` | `*Zval` | Non-nullable scalars follow ZPP as above. Other types reject null |
| `nullable + as = .zval` | `*Zval` | Call `is(.null)` on the borrowed zval |
| `optional + as = .zval`, optionally with `nullable` | `?*Zval` | Outer `null` means omission. Add `nullable` to preserve PHP null. Otherwise the rules above apply |

`as = .zval` takes precedence when selecting the result type and skips the `Nullable(T)` wrapper. The specification still checks the underlying type.

Each row below pairs one PHP declaration with the specification for its first argument. The last column is the Zig value read from `args[0]`.

## [.int]($heading.id('expect-int'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeInt(int $n): void` | `.{ .int = .{} }` | `i64` |
| `function byteValue(int $n): int` | `.{ .int = .{ .as = .u8 } }` | `u8` |
| `function optionalInt(int $n = 0): void` | `.{ .int = .{ .optional = true } }` | `?i64` |
| `function nullableInt(?int $n): void` | `.{ .int = .{ .nullable = true } }` | `phpz.Nullable(i64)` |
| `function optionalNullableInt(?int $n = null): void` | `.{ .int = .{ .optional = true, .nullable = true } }` | `?phpz.Nullable(i64)` |
| `function inspectInt(?int $n): void` | `.{ .int = .{ .nullable = true, .as = .zval } }` | `*Zval` |

```zig
pub fn byteValue(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !u8 {
    const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{.{ .int = .{ .as = .u8 } }}, {});
    return args[0];
}
```

```php
var_dump(byteValue(255)); // int(255)
optionalInt();
nullableInt(null);
optionalNullableInt();
optionalNullableInt(null);
```

Both `takeInt` and `byteValue` declare PHP `int`. The `.u8` specification additionally checks the range 0–255. Calling `byteValue(256)` raises `ValueError`. The stub default in `optionalInt` is metadata: the handler supplies zero with `args[0] orelse 0` when the slot is absent.

## [.float]($heading.id('expect-float'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeFloat(float $n): void` | `.{ .float = .{} }` | `f64` |
| `function takeFloat32(float $n): void` | `.{ .float = .{ .as = .f32 } }` | `f32` |

```php
takeFloat(3);
takeFloat32(1.25);
```

`.float = .{}` reads `f64` and `.float = .{ .as = .f32 }` reads `f32`. Both accept PHP ints. Weak calls also accept other ZPP-convertible scalars. The `f32` range check runs after conversion. Options combine as shown above.

## [.bool]($heading.id('expect-bool'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeFlag(bool $flag): void` | `.{ .bool = .{} }` | `bool` |

```php
takeFlag(false);
```

`.bool = .{}` returns a Zig `bool`. Weak calls use ZPP scalar conversions. Strict calls accept only PHP `true` and `false`. Arrays and other non-scalars are rejected.

## [.string]($heading.id('expect-string'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeBytes(string $s): void` | `.{ .string = .{} }` | `[]const u8` |
| `function takeZendString(string $s): void` | `.{ .string = .{ .as = .str } }` | `*zend.String` |
| `function takeCString(string $s): void` | `.{ .string = .{ .as = .cstr } }` | `[:0]const u8` |

```php
takeBytes("a\0b");
takeZendString("phpz");
takeCString("a\0b");
```

`.string = .{}` borrows a `[]const u8` of arbitrary bytes with no UTF-8 guarantee. `.string = .{ .as = .str }` borrows a `*zend.String`. To keep either past the source value's lifetime, see [Strings]($link.page('reference/types/strings')).

`.string = .{ .as = .cstr }` borrows a `[:0]const u8`, just like the corresponding typed parameter. It supports `optional` and `nullable` without allocating or retaining the string. The slice preserves embedded NULs. A C string function passed its `.ptr` sees only bytes before the first NUL.

## [.array]($heading.id('expect-array'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeArray(array $items): void` | `.{ .array = .{} }` | `*zend.Array` |
| `function inspectArray(array $items): void` | `.{ .array = .{ .as = .zval } }` | `*Zval` |

```php
takeArray([10, 20]);
inspectArray(["count" => 2]);
```

`.array = .{}` borrows a `*zend.Array`. `.array = .{ .as = .zval }` borrows a `*Zval` from which `array()` obtains a `Zval.Array` view. Neither separates shared arrays automatically. See [Arrays]($link.page('reference/types/arrays')) before modifying one.

## [.object]($heading.id('expect-object'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeObject(object $obj): void` | `.{ .object = .{} }` | `*zend.Object` |
| `function takeUser(User $user): void` | `.{ .object = .{ .instanceof = true } }` | `*zend.Object` |

```php
takeObject(new stdClass());
takeUser(new User());
```

Use `instanceof = true` to require a class or interface. Supply its class entry at the matching position in the runtime tuple:

```zig
const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
    .{ .object = .{ .instanceof = true } },
}, .{
    .{ .class = UserClass.entry },
});

const user: *phpz.zend.Object = args[0];
```

Use `{}` when a specification needs no runtime data. For an int followed by an object, pass `.{ {}, .{ .class = UserClass.entry } }`.

## [.resource]($heading.id('expect-resource'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/** @param resource $r */ function takeResource($r): void` | `.{ .resource = .{} }` | `*zend.Resource` |
| `/** @param resource $r */ function takeStream($r): void` | `.{ .resource = .{ .as = .stream } }` | `*phpz.Stream` |

```php
$stream = fopen("php://memory", "w+");
takeResource($stream);
takeStream($stream);
fclose($stream);
```

`.resource = .{}` borrows a `*zend.Resource` and checks only the PHP resource kind. `.resource = .{ .as = .stream }` checks for a regular or persistent PHP stream and borrows a `*phpz.Stream` (see [PHP streams]($link.page('reference/runtime/streams'))). For a custom payload, use its `Resource.Typed(...).fetch()` to verify the type and that the resource is open. See [PHP resources]($link.page('reference/types').ref('php-resources')).

## [.reference]($heading.id('expect-reference'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeRef(mixed &$v): void` | `.{ .reference = .{} }` | `*zend.Reference` |
| `function optionalRef(mixed &$v = null): void` | `.{ .reference = .{ .optional = true } }` | `?*zend.Reference` |
| `function inspectIntRef(int &$v): void` | `.{ .reference = .{ .kind = .int, .as = .value } }` | `*Zval` |
| `function inspectRef(mixed &$v): void` | `.{ .reference = .{ .as = .zval } }` | `*Zval` |

```php
$n = 7;
inspectIntRef($n);
optionalRef();
```

With `.as = .value` the zval points to the referenced value. With `.as = .zval` it points to the outer reference. Pass a PHP variable when supplying a by-reference argument.

## [.mixed]($heading.id('expect-mixed'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeMixed(mixed $v): void` | `.{ .mixed = .{} }` | `*Zval` |
| `function optionalMixed(mixed $v = null): void` | `.{ .mixed = .{ .optional = true } }` | `?*Zval` |
| `function takeUnion(int\|string $v): void` | `.{ .mixed = .{ .one_of = &.{ .int, .string } } }` | `Union` |
| `function optionalUnion(int\|string $v = 0): void` | `.{ .mixed = .{ .optional = true, .one_of = &.{ .int, .string } } }` | `?Union` |

```php
takeMixed(null);
takeUnion(42);
takeUnion("phpz");
optionalUnion();
```

`Union` here is `phpz.Mixed(&.{ .int, .string })`. Switch on `.int` or `.string` to use the payload. An optional union can also be omitted. The tags `.mixed`, `.reference`, `.callable` and `.variadic` cannot be members of `one_of`.

## [.callable]($heading.id('expect-callable'))

| PHP stub signature | Zig specification in `expectArgs` | Type of `args[0]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `function takeCallback(callable $cb): void` | `.{ .callable = .{} }` | `*Zval` |
| `function optionalCallback(callable $cb = UNKNOWN): void` | `.{ .callable = .{ .optional = true } }` | `?*Zval` |
| `function nullableCallback(?callable $cb): void` | `.{ .callable = .{ .nullable = true } }` | `phpz.Nullable(*Zval)` |
| `function optionalNullableCallback(?callable $cb = null): void` | `.{ .callable = .{ .optional = true, .nullable = true } }` | `?phpz.Nullable(*Zval)` |
| `function resolveCallback(callable $cb): void` | `.{ .callable = .{ .resolve = true } }` | `*Zval` |

```php
takeCallback("strlen");
optionalCallback();
optionalNullableCallback(null);
resolveCallback(static fn () => 42);
```

`UNKNOWN` is a PHP stub marker for an optional parameter whose default is handled by the extension. For `optionalCallback()`, the handler receives Zig `null`. For `nullableCallback(null)`, it receives `.null`.

For `resolve`, supply writable storage for a `zend.Callable`:

```zig
var target: phpz.zend.Callable = .nil;
const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
    .{ .callable = .{ .resolve = true } },
}, .{
    .{ .out = &target },
});

// args[0] borrows the *Zval. target now holds the resolved callable.
```

## [Accepting null]($heading.id('expect-null'))

For a nullable integer, use `function nullableInt(?int $n): void` from the [.int examples]($link.ref('expect-int')). To branch on null as a union member, use `.null` inside `one_of`:

```php
function classify(int|string|null $value): string {}
```

```zig
pub fn classify(ctx: phpz.Ctx) ![]const u8 {
    const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
        .{ .mixed = .{ .one_of = &.{ .int, .string, .null } } },
    }, {});
    return switch (args[0]) {
        .int => "integer",
        .string => "string",
        .null => "null",
    };
}
```

```php
echo classify(null); // null
```

# [Optional and nullable arguments]($heading.id('missing-and-null'))

The manual handler for `function choose(?int $value = null): ?int` can read both states from one argument slot:

```zig
const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
    .{ .int = .{ .optional = true, .nullable = true } },
}, {});
if (args[0]) |provided| {
    switch (provided) {
        .null => ctx.ret(.null, {}),
        .value => |value| ctx.ret(.int, value),
    }
} else {
    ctx.ret(.int, 0);
}
```

This handler body handles an optional `?int`. Omission returns zero, explicit null returns null and an integer returns its own value.

## [Handling defaults in Zig]($heading.id('handle-default-values'))

Declare the default in the stub and handle omission in Zig. For example, the skeleton implements `Counter::__construct(int $n = 0)` with `n: ?i64` and `n orelse 0`.

With `?i64`, omitting the trailing argument gives the handler Zig `null`. Passing PHP `null` in weak mode emits a deprecation and passes `0` to the handler, just like an explicit zero. In strict mode, passing `null` raises `TypeError`. To handle omission, explicit null and an integer separately, declare `?int $n = null` in the stub and use `?phpz.Nullable(i64)` in Zig.

# [Named and variadic arguments]($heading.id('named-and-variadic-arguments'))

Zend assigns named arguments to positions using parameter names from the stub. If a caller skips an optional parameter and names a later one, Zend may fill the earlier slot with its stub default. `optional` tests presence in the current frame, so a filled slot counts as present. Extra named variadic arguments are held in a separate table outside `args()` and `argCount()`.

## [Collecting and validating variadic arguments]($heading.id('variadic-values'))

This complete handler implements `function countPositive(int ...$values): int` and accepts positional and named arguments:

```zig
pub fn countPositive(values: phpz.Variadic) !usize {
    var count: usize = 0;
    var it = values.iterator();
    while (it.next()) |entry| {
        const value = try entry.expect(.{ .int = .{} }, {});
        if (value > 0) count += 1;
    }
    return count;
}
```

```php
var_dump(countPositive(1, -2, extra: 3)); // int(2)
var_dump(countPositive());                // int(0)
```

`Variadic` must come last. For manual parsing, collect the tail with a single trailing `.{ .variadic = .{} }`. For example, this handler implements `function countTail(string $label, mixed ...$values): int`:

```zig
pub fn countTail(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !usize {
    const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
        .{ .string = .{} },
        .{ .variadic = .{} },
    }, {});
    return args[1].len();
}
```

By default both positional and extra named arguments are accepted. Before processing entries, use `try values.check(.positional_only)`, `try values.check(.named_only)` or `try values.check(.both)` to enforce a `phpz.Variadic.Mode`. Disallowed sources raise an error. The mode applies only to the tail. Arguments matching fixed parameter names are unrestricted and an empty tail satisfies all three modes.

`len()` counts the entire tail. `positional` borrows a slice and `named` borrows an optional table. `iterator()` visits positional arguments first and then extra named arguments in table order. Each `Entry.value` borrows a `*Zval` that preserves reference containers. `name()` returns an optional name. Its `identifier` is a `phpz.ArgIdentifier` with a one-based `.position` or a `.name`.

`entry.expect(spec, runtime)` uses the single-argument rules for weak and strict conversion, nullable types, unions, references, class constraints and runtime callable data. Conversion updates the original zval. Named-argument errors identify `($name)`. Only entries passed to `expect` receive this validation. An element specification cannot be optional or contain another `.variadic`.

`Variadic` borrows its arguments and named table for the duration of the call. Never free or structurally modify that table. Copy or retain individual values to extend their lifetime.

Without a trailing `.variadic`, `expectArgs` rejects extra named arguments. At the lower level, `extraNamedArgs()` gives access to the named table, `args()` borrows the current frame's argument slice, and `expectNoExtraNamedArgs()` rejects a named tail. Check every argument yourself when using these APIs.

## [Passing variadic arguments by reference]($heading.id('variadic-references'))

The stub’s `&...` asks PHP to pass each caller variable by reference. Zig receives one `phpz.Variadic`. Copying each entry with `Zval.raw.copy` preserves the reference container. Inserting that copy transfers its reference to the result array, under the original name or at the next numeric index.

```php
function collectReferenceArguments(mixed &...$args): array {}
```

```zig
pub fn collectReferenceArguments(ctx: phpz.Ctx, args: phpz.Variadic) !void {
    const result = phpz.Zval.Array.empty(ctx.retval.ptr());
    var it = args.iterator();
    while (it.next()) |entry| {
        var copy = phpz.Zval.raw.undef;
        phpz.Zval.raw.copy(&copy, entry.value.ptr());
        errdefer phpz.Zval.raw.release(&copy);
        if (entry.name()) |name| {
            result.set(.mixed, name, &copy);
        } else {
            try result.append(.mixed, &copy);
        }
    }
}
```

```php
$a = 1;
$b = 2;
$refs = collectReferenceArguments($a, extra: $b);
$refs[0] = 10;
$b = 20;
var_dump($a, $refs["extra"]); // int(10), int(20)
```

# [Error propagation]($heading.id('error-propagation'))

Use `try` to propagate parsing and call errors to the wrapper. Parsers return `PhpException` or `UnwindExit` through `phpz.exception.Error` and preserve PHP's pending state. Ordinary Zig errors become PHP `Error` objects. `UnwindExit` signals an internal exit, which may follow `exit`, `die` or deferred OOM. Propagate it and `ZendBailout` after native cleanup. See [Cleanup rules]($link.page('advanced/bailout').ref('cleanup-rules')) for `OutOfMemory`. An error must end the normal return path.
